---
//! Answers for people choosing a streaming setup. Platform and network
//! limits follow README.md, docs/parity.md and docs/networking.md.
import Testers from './Testers.astro';
import { onGitHub } from '../lib/repo';
---

<section class="section" id="faq">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="head">
      <span class="eyebrow">FAQ</span>
      <h2 class="title">Before you stream</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="questions">
      <details>
        <summary><h3>Can I use Helio for remote desktop gaming?</h3></summary>
        <p>
          Yes. Helio Host streams the desktop, games included, to Helio on another computer: measured at 3024×1890 and
          120 fps, with HEVC, H.264 or AV1, HDR, 4:4:4 colour and up to 7.1 sound. Controllers and rumble need a Windows
          host. See the <a href="#latency">benchmarks and their test conditions</a>.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary><h3>Is Helio an alternative to Moonlight and Sunshine/Apollo?</h3></summary>
        <p>
          Yes, with its own protocol, so install it on both computers: it does not connect to Sunshine or Apollo, and
          Moonlight does not connect to Helio Host. See the
          <a href={onGitHub('docs/parity.md')}>feature comparison with Moonlight and Sunshine/Apollo</a>.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary><h3>Which computers can host and receive a stream?</h3></summary>
        <p>
          macOS 14 or later, Windows 10 and 11, and Linux under X11 or Wayland, as client and as host; phones, tablets
          and TVs are out of scope. On a Linux host under sway or Hyprland, the client cannot send keyboard or mouse
          input. See the <a href="#platforms">platform requirements</a>.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary><h3>Do I need a VPN or port forwarding to stream over the internet?</h3></summary>
        <p>
          Usually neither. Pair once on your local network, or through a VPN such as Tailscale when away from the host:
          Helio then links the host’s web UI to type the PIN in. Internet streaming is on by default, finding paired
          hosts through sealed records on the BitTorrent DHT and connecting directly. There is no relay: if symmetric NAT
          on both sides blocks it, forward UDP port 47800 to the host. See
          <a href={onGitHub('docs/networking.md')}>internet setup and network limits</a>.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary><h3>How does Helio stay up to date?</h3></summary>
        <p>
          The window says when a release is out. <strong>Install and Restart</strong> downloads it from GitHub, checks
          it, installs it the way you installed the app and opens Helio again. Nothing downloads until you choose. See
          <a href={onGitHub('docs/usage.md#updates')}>how each system installs an update</a>.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary><h3>How do I uninstall Helio?</h3></summary>
        <p>
          On Windows, <strong>Settings &gt; Apps &gt; Installed apps</strong>, then Helio, then
          <strong>Uninstall</strong>. With Homebrew, <code>brew uninstall --cask helio</code>; on Linux,
          <code>install.sh --uninstall</code> from the download. Pairings and settings stay for a later install. See
          <a href={onGitHub('docs/install.md#uninstalling')}>what to delete to remove them too</a>, and the rest of a
          DMG install on a Mac.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary><h3>Is Helio free and open source?</h3></summary>
        <p>
          Yes. Helio is free to download and use, and its source is under the GPL-3.0 license.
          <a href="#install">Download Helio</a> for your system.
        </p>
      </details>
    </div>
    <Testers />
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .questions {
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  details {
    padding-block: 12px;
  }

  summary {
    color: var(--text);
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  summary::marker {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  h3 {
    display: inline;
    font-size: 1rem;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }

  details p {
    margin-top: 12px;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }
</style>
